b $4000 Display File
D $4000 There is no loading screen. The tape loads through the ROM loader,
. and leaves the display file mostly clear. The picture below is the opening
. screen as drawn by the BASIC program (captured after autorun reaches ROM
. PAUSE at #N($1F3D,$04,$04)).
D $4000 #PUSHS #OPENINGPOKES
. #UDGTABLE
. { =h Opening screen (as drawn by the BASIC program). }
. { #SCR$02(opening) }
. UDGTABLE#
. #POPS
@ $4000 label=DisplayFile
  $4000,$1800,$20 Pixels.
  $5800,$0300,$20 Attributes.

i $5B00
i $5C00

b $5CCB BASIC Program
@ $5CCB label=BasicProgram
D $5CCB #HTML(Velnor's Lair by Derek Brewster, published by Neptune Computing
. in 1983. It is the first of his two "Myths and Legends of Caraland"
. games; <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/7157"
. rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a> also notes
. the Quicksilva reissue and the sequel, Kentilla.)
D $5CCB #HTML(The whole game is a ROM-loaded BASIC program. The tape header
. names it "VELNOR", sets autorun at line 4860, and gives a program length of
. #N($93D2,$04,$04) and a data length of #N($A112,$04,$04). There is no
. custom tape loader.)
D $5CCB #HTML(Line 4860 is <code>POKE D,F: GO TO A</code> with the saved
. variables <code>D=23728</code> (#SKOOLKID$5CB0(NMIADD)), <code>F=4</code>
. and <code>A=1</code>, so it writes #N($04) to NMIADD and starts at line 5.
. Line 4870 (<code>PRINT USR TU</code>) is not the autorun path.)
D $5CCB #HTML(Line 6035 POKEs a #N($10)-byte routine at #R$FF58 that writes
. attribute #N($38) across the screen; line 6330 calls it with
. <code>RANDOMIZE USR 65368</code>. That code is not on the tape and is not
. present in this snapshot.)
B $5CCB,$0013 Line 5.
